  "content": {
    "id": "p51-q21-group-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "True or False? Let $A$, $B$ and $C$ be sets in a universal set $U$.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p51-q21-response",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$A\\cup(B\\cap C)=(A\\cup B)\\cap(A\\cup C)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. An element in $A$ belongs to both sides. An element outside $A$ belongs to either side exactly when it is in both $B$ and $C$. Thus both sides contain the same elements.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p51-q22-response",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$A\\cap(B\\cup C)=(A\\cap B)\\cup(A\\cap C)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. Both sides contain exactly the elements of $A$ that also belong to $B$ or $C$, including those in both.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p51-q23-response",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$(A\\cap B)\\cap C=A\\cap(B\\cup C)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "False. The left side requires membership in all three sets; the right requires membership in $A$ and at least one of $B$ or $C$. For $U=\\{1\\}$, $A=B=\\{1\\}$ and $C=\\varnothing$,\n$$\\begin{align*}(A\\cap B)\\cap C&=\\varnothing,\\\\A\\cap(B\\cup C)&=\\{1\\}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p51-q24-response",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$\\overline{A\\cup B\\cup C}=\\overline A\\cap\\overline B\\cap\\overline C$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. Being outside $A\\cup B\\cup C$ means being outside each of $A$, $B$ and $C$. These are exactly the elements common to all three complements.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      }
    ]
  },
